Here's how I'm thinking it might work:

A request for `GET /search?category=bears` comes in. Counterfact will look for a `GET` function exported from `paths/search.ts`. If it doesn't find one, and the proxy is on, it will forward the request to the proxy. Then it will use the response to generate the missing code.

```ts
export function GET($) {
  if ($.query?.category === "bears") {
    return $.response
      .status(200)
      .body({ category: "bears", items: ["grizzly", "polar", "teddy"] });
  }
  return $.proxyAndRecord();
}
```

Another request for `GET /search?category=bears` comes in. The proxy is still on, but now Counterfact doesn't bother contacting the proxy. It returns the previously hard-coded result.

A request for `GET /search?category=beets` comes in. Now the code falls through to `return $.proxyAndRecord()`. It forwards the request to the proxy, returns the response from the proxy, and updates the code.

```ts
export function GET($) {
  if ($.query?.category === "bears") {
    return $.response
      .status(200)
      .body({ category: "bears", items: ["grizzly", "polar", "teddy"] });
  }
  if ($.query?.category === "beets") {
    return $.response.status(200).body({
      category: "beets",
      items: ["avalanche", "bull's blood", "chioggia"],
    });
  }
  return $.proxyAndRecord();
}
```

Working this way will accumulate a whole bunch of if statements and repetitive code. If we're happy to record and play back responses from the proxy, that's fine. We can edit the code to add a fallback for cases that weren't encountered while recording the proxy responses.

```ts
export function GET($) {
  if ($.query?.category === "bears") {
    return $.response
      .status(200)
      .body({ category: "bears", items: ["grizzly", "polar", "teddy"] });
  }
  if ($.query?.category === "beets") {
    return $.response.status(200).body({
      category: "beets",
      items: ["avalanche", "bull's blood", "chioggia"],
    });
  }
  return $.response.status(200).body({
    category: $.query.category,
    items: ["item 1", "item 2", "item 3"],
  });
}
```

Or maybe now that we understand the shape of the response we can get rid of hard-coded values. Instead we'll generate a response that's the right shape and real enough for our testing purposes. 

```ts
export function GET($) {
  return $.status(200).body({
    category: $.query.category,
    items: new Array(3)
      .fill(null)
      .map((_, i) => `${$.query.category} item ${i + 1}`),
  });
}
```

And eventually start exploring some of the more advanced features of Counterfact, like the context object.

```ts
export function GET($) {

  const numberOfItems = $.context.numberOfItemsPerCategory ?? 3; 

  return $.status(200).body({
    category: $.query.category,
    items: new Array(numberOfItems)
      .fill(null)
      .map((_, i) => `${$.query.category} item ${i + 1}`),
  });
}
