For example, a request object is currently passed as the first argument.

```ts
export const GET: HTTP_GET = ({ response, context }) => {
  context.increment();
  return response[200].json(String(context.count))
};
```

It could instead be passed as the `this` argument.

```ts
export const GET: HTTP_GET = () => {
  this.context.increment();
  return this.response[200].json(String(context.count))
};
```

The reason being **it will make using autocomplete more useful**. Type `this.` in the IDE and all of the properties will show up under autocomplete.

If you don't like seeing `this.` everywhere, you can make the second block almost identical to the first with one "extra" line of code. 

```ts
export const GET: HTTP_GET = () => {
  const { response, context } = this;
  context.increment();
  return response[200].json(String(context.count))
};
```

An exported object named `MODEL` will appear in the request arguments as `model`. 

```ts
import type { HTTP_GET, HTTP_PUT } from "../path-types/hello.types.js";

class HelloModel {
   message: "hello world"
}

export const MODEL = new HelloModel();

export const GET:HTTP_GET = ({ model }) => {
    return model.message;
}

export const PUT:HTTP_PUT = ({ body, model, response }) => {
    model.message = body;
    return response[201];
}
```

- The model is loaded the first time Counterfact encounters a file. 
- The model stays in memory and is not affected by hot-reloading.
- The `context` object is deprecated. `model` is easier to use and a better name.
- If `./paths/a/b/c/d.js` does not export a `MODEL`, Counterfact will look for `MODEL` in `./paths/a/b/c.js`, `./paths/a/b.js`, `./paths/a.js`, `./paths.js` and ultimately fall back to `{}`.

In TypeScript, every path file MUST export `MODEL` so that `/path-types/**/*.type.ts` can find and use it. 

```ts
export { MODEL } from '../parent.js'; 
```

Somewhat speculative. In a case like this calculating both a text and a JSON body is wasteful, as we're only going to use one of them. 

```js
export async function GET() {
  return response[200].text(calculateText()).json(calculateJson()) ;
}
```

It's easy to address. We can optionally pass a function wherever a body is expected, and lazily evaluate that function. 

```js
export async function GET() {
  return response[200].text(() => calculateText()).json(() => calculateJson()) ;
}
```

Also, the body could be a ReadableStream or a generator. 

These are edge cases. I haven't yet run into a need to support them. If and when we do, I want to make sure we have a plan and don't have to go back to the drawing board.

